{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Organizations emits CloudTrail events for account membership changes - AWS","context":"AWS Organizations now emits CloudTrail events to the management account whenever accounts join or leave the organization. These new events, AccountJoinedOrganization and AccountDepartedOrganization, provide enhanced visibility into organizational membership changes. The AccountJoinedOrganization event captures how an account joined (Created or Invited) and the join timestamp. The AccountDepartedOrganization event records how an account departed (Left for voluntary departures, Removed for account removal by the management account, or Cleaned for permanently closed accounts) and the departure timestamp. These events can be used to create CloudWatch alarms or Amazon EventBridge rules for real-time notifications, enabling rapid response to suspicious organizational changes. These CloudTrail events are available in all commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and China Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-organizations-cloudtrail/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Selectively log network activity events by identity in AWS CloudTrail - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced enhanced event filtering for network activity events related to Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) endpoints in CloudTrail. This new feature allows customers to selectively log network activity events based on the identity of the IAM user making the API call. Users can configure selectors to log only specific events, such as access denied events, when the calling identity is not on a trusted list. This helps in reducing logging costs and filtering out routine traffic from trusted identities. The UserIdentity filtering feature can be used to focus on network activity event logging for security scenarios and detect potential data exfiltration attempts through VPC endpoints. Users can combine UserIdentity conditions with existing fields like eventName or vpcEndpointId for fine-grained control over what gets recorded. The feature is available via the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs in all AWS Regions where CloudTrail network activity events are supported. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail user guide or read the AWS Blog on Network Activity Events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-cloudtrail-filter-useridentity-advance-selectors/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail GetEventSelectors behavior for organization trails from member accounts"}
